jeering taken during a session of the  Home Affairs Select Committee when you  were hearing from officials also from  the Immigration Minister can you just  explain what was going through your mind  here and why it was the end of a sort of  series of questions where we weren't  getting answers and where the home  office just seemed to be in a complete  mess about arrangements that may need to  be in place for just five months time  and this is about what should happen if  there is no deal for EU citizens both  those who are here already and where the  government's rightly said they will have  their rights respected and will be able  to continue to stay and work but also  for people arriving for the first time  perhaps coming to work and the  government seem to be saying that free  movement would end straightaway on day  one but also that there wouldn't be any  checks on people arriving but then maybe  that there would be checks by employers  but then that they wouldn't be able to  differentiate between checks or new  arrivals and checks on people who've  lived here for a long time and it just  got so muddled and seemed to be so full  of contradictions  they thought we've only got five months  to go surely the home or office ought to  be better prepared and ought to at least  know one earth it's going to be doing  and we still don't know the answer to a  lot of those questions so who should be  responsible I mean I think it was quite  clear during the session that you were  becoming frustrated with the Immigration  Minister Caroline Noakes I mean do you  think she's the right person for the job  I think to be honest there is a conflict  here actually going on between the home  office and number 10 that the sense that  I've got and from some of the reports  and sort of clarifications since is that  number ten is still trying to maintain  that free movement will end on day one  if there is no deal the home office in  practice knows that they've got no new  system that they would have in place no  changes that they could possibly  implement in time and I have the feeling  that actually the Immigration Minister  you know more junior minister and the  officials are a bit caught in the  crossfire on that they know that there's  no sensible decisions being made no  proper planning being done and that's  why they were getting themselves into  such a tangle so I don't actually think  it was their fault so you think they're  effectively being hung out to dry by a  number tad  a conflict between you know number 10 on  the one hand the Home Secretary on the  other and nobody actually from making  proper clear decisions or being straight  with us and being clear about what the  consequences will be and the reason this  matters is obviously it's a big concern  for EU citizens who are here but there's  a lot of other issues for example what  the security arrangements going to be  what the security arrangements going to  be at the border if we don't have shared  information from the EU what are the  policing cooperation arrangements going  to be and we heard from senior police  officers about the downgrade in security  that we'll have the inability to be able  to arrest people who may have committed  crimes in other countries if we don't  have a proper deal in place in these  proper arrangements in place I mean it's  pretty clear that you're alarmed should  we say at the prospect of no deal  actually say five months to go and with  that in mind could you see yourselves  supporting a deal that Theresa May comes  back with well I think clearly no deal  is the worst situation for all of us but  and a bad deal is a better than no deal  but I don't think the country should be  forced into accepting a bad deal simply  because the Prime Minister and the  Conservative Party and if it is to come  deal with authorizing well that may be  the reality so that's why I think it's  really important that the government is  now preparing the option of being able  to extend article 50 because no deal we  clearly cannot accept it would be very  damaging for our security but a bad deal  something that's going to be bad for the  country for the long term we shouldn't  be forced into that because we've run  out of time because of the chaos and  confusion so I do think now this should  be preparations made so that if the  Prime Minister doesn't get a good deal  before Christmas we don't just get timed  out we don't just end up in a mess and  we do make proper sensible plans now to  extend article 50 in those circumstances  to make sure that we can get an  arrangement that's in the best interest  of the country what about the idea of a  second referendum I don't think that  should be the focus at the moment I  think the focus should be on the deal I  think that the Labour Party has the  right position which is look what you've  got to focus on is is trying to get the  right deal of course you can't rule out  things in this very case you'd prefer  see an extension article fit but I think  the right thing we should be focusing on  is trying to get a good deal but if that  is not working out extending article 50  so there's a chance to look at this  again to renegotiate and to properly get  it right I mean a report that Theresa  May is close to agreeing apart that  withdrawal agreement this idea that the  whole of the UK could be kept in an  effective customs union with EU is that  something you'd support I think being a  customs union is really important  personally I think it should be a front  stop not just a back stop because I  think this is good for Yorkshire  manufacturing it's about manufacturing  jobs and trade but you must be focused  the idea even if it's so I hope that  they have made progress to agree a  customs union I think that would be a  step forward but this only still seems  to be about the the withdrawal agreement  in the temporary arrangements not about  the long term plan we need some clarity  about what our arrangements and  relationship will be for the future so  we're not going into this blind and we  also I think need some reassurance on  these security issues nobody's talking  about what the security arrangements  will be we've heard that even if there's  a deal we could still lose access to  some of the really important criminal  databases but if that downgrades our  security we need answers for that as  well so I want to hear the prime  minister talking about security side not  just the trade side and now what to take  you back to immigration where we started  the interview because it does feel as if  policy is somewhat chaotic should we say  at the minute and we've had of course  the wind rush allegations the windows  scandal that happened we've also had at  the recent scandal of some people being  forced to give DNA samples or asked to  give DNA samples is it the Home Office  that's not fit for purpose I think  that's a really big question and I am  very worried actually about the state of  the home office I think we had the Alex  Allen review that was published or the  executive summary that was published  last week which did raise questions  about the advice that was given to Amber  Radha's former Home Secretary but of  course there are also really  responsibilities that ministers have to  take about the state of the department  as well but I think overall  we've just had one set of mistakes after  another from the Home Office the reason  this matters is because these are the  kinds of decisions that have huge  implications for people's lives and  really devastating effects for people's  lives when the Home Office gets things  wrong we're going to be hearing from the  Home Secretary in a few weeks time we're  also going to be hearing from the  permanent secretary as well that the  head official in the department so we  will be putting those questions to them  I mean do you think there is an argument  for example that the Home Office should  be split up and immigration should be  moved to different Department is it too  big personally I do think that is  something that should be looked at but  it's not something we've discussed on  the Home Affairs Select Committee so  it's not a committee view but I think  when you've got all of the issues around  crime and policing we concluded recently  that there hasn't been enough home  office leadership on crime and tackling  crime for example and and working with  the police to support the police and  then you've got all of the issues around  immigration as well so you could look at  different structures but as I said  having not we've not addressed this as a  committee so we don't have  recommendations to make at this point  and I want to talk to you about  something a little bit different in tax  cuts you have calls voted in a very  different way to your party's leadership  about the tax cuts in the budget and why  did you do that and what was your  reasoning behind that well in April  there's a whole series of further cuts  that are going to be made for some of  the poorest families in the country cuts  to in the value of child benefit and  value of the tax credits and so you've  got some of the poorest people in the  country who are going to end up being  hundreds of pounds worse off  and yet the Chancellor's announced this  budget which gives big tax cuts to some  of the richest people in the country  people on a hundred thousand pounds a  year who are going to get hundreds of  pounds back in tax cuts at the same time  as taking away hundreds of pounds from  some of those on lowest income and I  just think that's wrong and we've got a  rising child poverty those in my  constituency who are facing real  hardship and what we need is a plan that  is fair

